Stray ground-truth intervention -- PASS

Unmark exactly one primitive's bRenderCustomDepth via the engine's own
setter, and require exactly that id's pixels to disappear from the mask.
This is the only check in the project that cannot be satisfied by
coherent-but-wrong data: every other one reads the buffer and asks whether
it looks right, this one CHANGES the world and requires the mask to follow.

Companion to inzoi-groundtruth-pass.log. That one proves it on UE5.6 +
Nanite, where the ids live in a separate R16G16_UINT colour target. This
one proves the same property on UE4, where they live in the depth-stencil's
stencil plane -- a different engine, a different carrier, the same test.

Note the direction of the second number. The OTHER slots GAIN pixels, they
do not merely stay still, and that is the correct signature rather than a
tolerance being met: the unmarked surface stops writing to CustomDepth, so
it stops occluding there, and whatever is behind it paints through its
silhouette. A mask that simply blanked the slot would leave the rest
unchanged.

Run: capture.py stray --seconds 120 --groundtruth   (2026-08-13)
